xecuted against `BookingTimeStatusDenormalized`. Authorization regressions in those branches (e.g. wrong parenthesization, missing `teamId`/`isTeamBooking` clauses, leak across org boundaries) will not be caught by this suite."}, {"file": "packages/lib/server/service/insightsBooking.ts", "line": 68, "severity": "low", "category": "style", "title": "Dead branches in getBaseConditions", "body": "`getAuthorizationConditions` is typed `Promise<Prisma.Sql>` and is never null (it always returns either `NOTHING_CONDITION` or a real SQL fragment), so the `else if (authConditions)` branch in `getBaseConditions` is unreachable — `Prisma.Sql` is an object and therefore always truthy. The fallback `else { return NOTHING_CONDITION; }` branch is similarly unreachable. This is harmless dead code but it misleads readers about what can be returned and what combinations are possible."}, {"file": "packages/lib/server/service/insightsBooking.ts", "line": 145, "severity": "medium", "category": "data", "title": "Org scope user list excludes users only on the org membership", "body": "`buildOrgAuthorizationCondition` derives `userIdsFromOrg` from `MembershipRepository.findAllByTeamIds({ teamIds })` where `teamIds` is `[orgId, ...childTeamIds]`. In practice this only returns users who have a membership in the org *or* one of its child teams — but anyone whose membership row points directly at the org but who has no child-team membership is still included, and conversely users who only sit on sub-teams are included. The more subtle problem is the inverse: a user who is *only* an org member via a direct org membership row with no team-level membership is included only because `orgId` is in `teamIds`. If the data ever drifts so the org itself is excluded from `teamIds` (or if the query is later changed to fetch only sub-team memberships to optimize), those users' non-team bookings will silently disappear from org-scope insights. The pre-existing object-based version had the same shape, so this is not a regression, but the SQL rewrite is a good opportunity to make the intent explicit (and to add an integration test for an org member without any sub-team membership)."}, {"file": "packages/lib/server/service/insightsBooking.ts", "line": 190, "severity": "low", "category": "perf", "title": "Team scope does not deduplicate userIdsFromTeam", "body": "`buildOrgAuthorizationCondition` deduplicates `userIdsFromOrg` via `Array.from(new Set(userIdsFromOrg))` before binding into `ANY(${uniqueUserIds})`. `buildTeamAuthorizationCondition` does the equivalent operation but skips the deduplication step (`const userIdsFromTeam = usersFromTeam.map((u) => u.userId);` is used directly). For a team with many members who happen to share membership rows the resulting `ANY(...)` array can balloon, growing the bound parameter size and the SQL plan unnecessarily. Make the two paths consistent."}, {"file": "packages/lib/server/service/__tests__/insightsBooking.integration-test.ts", "line": 199, "severity": "low", "category": "test_gap", "title": "No afterEach cleanup; failing tests leak database rows", "body": "Cleanup is implemented as a returned `cleanup()` function that each test must remember to call at the end of its body. If a test fails or throws before reaching the `await testData.cleanup()` line (and there is at least one such path — e.g. the new `$queryRaw` assertion in the `getBaseConditions` describe block, which calls cleanup only after the expect), the user/team/membership/booking/eventType rows it created persist in the shared database, polluting subsequent runs. Wrap `createTestData` in a `try/finally` or use `afterEach(() => testData?.cleanup())` to guarantee cleanup."}, {"file": "packages/lib/server/service/__tests__/insightsBooking.integration-test.ts", "line": 481, "severity": "low", "category": "test_gap", "title": "Integration test does not assert query column shape or count edge cases", "body": "The only end-to-end `$queryRaw` assertion in the suite (`expec